\DOC LOCAL_AMONTSQR_P384_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_AMONTSQR_P384_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances the {eventually} goal of a machine-code correctness proof across an
inlined almost-Montgomery-squaring subroutine, replacing the current symbolic
state by a successor state and adding the subroutine's result specification to
the assumptions.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument, an {int}, selects which of the available input/output
buffer-location variants of the subroutine step is used; the second, a
{string list}, names the result variable {t} and the input variable {u} whose
values the newly introduced assumptions talk about. Applied to a goal whose
conclusion is an {eventually} statement, it yields a single subgoal about a
fresh later state {s''}:
{
   A ?- eventually x86 (\s'. ...) s
   =============================================  LOCAL_AMONTSQR_P384_TAC n ["t"; "u"]
   A' ?- eventually x86 (\s'. ...) s''
}
\noindent Here {A'} retains the purely arithmetic/nonoverlapping assumptions of
{A} and drops all state-indexed assumptions about {s} (program-counter,
register, memory and {MAYCHANGE} facts), re-adding them restated at {s''}: a
new program-counter equation, the code-loaded fact, the register and
input/output memory readings, and a {MAYCHANGE} relation from the initial state
to {s''} extended with the frame components written by this step. {A'} further
contains a bound assumption of the form {t < 2 EXP 384}, a congruence
assumption relating {t} to the Montgomery-scaled square of {u}, and a
memory-read equation giving {t} as the contents of the destination buffer at
{s''}.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, the tactic exhibits exactly this single-subgoal
state-advancing behaviour, always on goals whose conclusion is such an
{eventually} statement.

\ENDDOC
